L42: ### 12.1 Introduction
L43: 
L44: This section is non-normative.
L45: 
L46: This specification introduces two related mechanisms, similar to HTTP session cookies, for storing name-value pairs on the client side. 【19†[COOKIES]】
L47: 
L48: The first is designed for scenarios where the user is carrying out a single transaction, but could be carrying out multiple transactions in different windows at the same time.
L49: Cookies don't really handle this case well. For example, a user could be buying plane tickets in two different windows, using the same site. If the site used cookies to keep track of which ticket the user was buying, then as the user clicked from page to page in both windows, the ticket currently being purchased would "leak" from one window to the other, potentially causing the user to buy two tickets for the same flight without noticing.
L50: To address this, this specification introduces the `【20†sessionStorage】` getter. Sites can add data to the session storage, and it will be accessible to any page from the same site opened in that window.
L51: 
L52: For example, a page could have a checkbox that the user ticks to indicate that they want insurance:
L53: 
L54:     `<label>
L55:      <input type="checkbox" onchange="sessionStorage.insurance = checked ? 'true' : ''">
L56:       I want insurance on this trip.
L57:     </label>`
L58: A later page could then check, from script, whether the user had checked the checkbox or not:
L59: 
L60:     `if (sessionStorage.insurance) { ... }`
L61: 
L62: If the user had multiple windows opened on the site, each one would have its own individual copy of the session storage object.
L63: The second storage mechanism is designed for storage that spans multiple windows, and lasts beyond the current session. In particular, web applications might wish to store megabytes of user data, such as entire user-authored documents or a user's mailbox, on the client side for performance reasons.
L64: 
L65: Again, cookies do not handle this case well, because they are transmitted with every request.
L66: 
L67: The `【21†localStorage】` getter is used to access a page's local storage area.
L68: The site at example.com can display a count of how many times the user has loaded its page by putting the following at the bottom of its page:
L69: 
L70:     `<p>
L71:       You have viewed this page
L72:       <span id="count">an untold number of</span>
L73:       time(s).
L74:     </p>
L75:     <script>
L76:       if (!localStorage.pageLoadCount)
L77:         localStorage.pageLoadCount = 0;
L78:       localStorage.pageLoadCount = parseInt(localStorage.pageLoadCount) + 1;
L79:       document.getElementById('count').textContent = localStorage.pageLoadCount;
L80:     </script>`
