L55: # Angular Signals
L56: 
L57: Angular Signals is a system that granularly tracks how and where your state is used throughout an application, allowing the framework to optimize rendering updates.
L58: 
L59: TIP: Check out Angular's 【34†Essentials】 before diving into this comprehensive guide.
L60: 
L61: ## On this page
L62: 
L63: arrow_upward_alt Back to the top
L64: ## 【35†What are signals?】
L65: 
L66: A signal is a wrapper around a value that notifies interested consumers when that value changes. Signals can contain any value, from primitives to complex data structures.
L67: 
L68: You read a signal's value by calling its getter function, which allows Angular to track where the signal is used.
L69: 
L70: Signals may be either writable or read-only.
L71: ### 【36†Writable signals】
L72: 
L73: Writable signals provide an API for updating their values directly. You create writable signals by calling the 【37†`signal`】 function with the signal's initial value:
L74: 
L75:     `const count = 【37†signal】(0);
L76: 
L77:     // Signals are getter functions - calling them reads their value.
L78:     console.log('The count is: ' + count());`
L79: 
L80: To change the value of a writable signal, either `.set()` it directly:
L81: 
L82:     `count.set(3);`
L83: or use the `.update()` operation to compute a new value from the previous one:
L84: 
L85:     `// Increment the count by 1.
L86:     count.update((value) => value + 1);`
L87: 
L88: Writable signals have the type 【38†`WritableSignal`】.
L89: #### 【39†Converting writable signals to readonly】
L90: 【38†`WritableSignal`】 provides an `asReadonly()` method that returns a readonly version of the signal. This is useful when you want to expose a signal's value to consumers without allowing them to modify it directly:
L91: 
L92:     `@【40†Service】()
L93:     export class CounterState {
L94:       // Private writable state
L95:       private readonly _count = 【37†signal】(0);
L96: 
L97:       readonly count = this._count.asReadonly(); // public readonly
L98: 
L99:       increment() {
L100:         this._count.update((v) => v + 1);
L101:       }
L102:     }
L103: 
L104:     @【41†Component】({/* ... */})
L105:     export class AwesomeCounter {
L106:       state = inject(CounterState);
L107: 
L108:       count = this.state.count; // can read but not modify
L109: 
L110:       increment() {
